{
  "dom_id": "step-q82572",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14116716/assets/ce68b2f27b88bd43d6eebfa5380a22c00e15e4194e0ad45e6778f761667a3ceb.png)\n\nFor the function $f:(0,\\infty )\\to R$ defined by $f(x)=x-2$, which of the following statements are true? \n\n- The function is an injection\n- The function is a surjection\n- The function is a bijection",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a function $f:X\\to Y$ is \n\n- an injection if $∀\\,{x}_{1},{x}_{2}\\in X,$ whenever $f({x}_{1})=f({x}_{2}),$ we have ${x}_{1}={x}_{2},$\n\n- a surjection if $∀\\,y\\in Y,$ $∃\\,x\\in X$ such that $f(x)=y,$\n\n- a bijection if it is both a surjection and an injection.\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Any horizontal line (like the one depicted below) crosses the graph of $f(x)$ at most once.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14116716/assets/2ff4c0183b91aa23d780f665aa0f038b374583e9f24e245951c4c69ea5f2e5b8.png)\n\n           \n\n- Statement II is false. There is no value $x$ for which $f(x)=-2\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false because the function is not a surjection.\n\n Therefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
